        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-409",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "JWE decompression bomb (zip bomb) in jose/jwe.py: attacker-controlled `jwe_str` (parameter of the EntryPoint method `decrypt`, jose/jwe.py:60) flows through `_jwe_compact_deserialize(jwe_str)` (line 79, which unpacks attacker-controlled `header`, `cipher_text`, `iv`, `auth_tag`) into `_decrypt_and_auth(...)` (line 178), and the resulting `plain_text` is passed to `_decompress(header.get(\"zip\"), plain_text)` at line 187, which reaches `zlib.decompress(compressed)` at jose/jwe.py:453 with NO `max_length`/size cap and no post-decompression length check. The attacker also controls the `zip` header value selecting the DEF (deflate) branch (line 452 `zip == ZIPS.DEF`). Critically, the authentication at line 240 is not a barrier for public-key JWE algorithms (RSA-OAEP / RSA1_5 / ECDH-ES are in ALGORITHMS.SUPPORTED): any attacker who knows the recipient's PUBLIC key can legitimately produce a correctly-authenticated JWE containing a highly-compressed payload, so the tag check passes and decompression proceeds. A ~50KB token can expand to multiple GB, causing unbounded memory allocation and denial of service.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Independently re-derived and confirmed. jose/jwe.py:453 calls zlib.decompress(compressed) with no max_length and no post-decompression length check; a repo-wide grep for max_length|decompressobj|MAX_|LIMIT across jose/ finds only _MAX_RECOVERY_ATTEMPTS in rsa_backend.py:52, so no cap exists anywhere in the library. The attacker-controlled flow is present in real source: decrypt(jwe_str, key) at jwe.py:60 -> _jwe_compact_deserialize(jwe_str) at line 79 (which imposes NO length limit on jwe_str, lines 256-327) -> _decrypt_and_auth(...) at line 178 -> _decompress(header.get(\"zip\"), plain_text) at line 187 -> zlib.decompress at 453, with the DEF branch selected by the attacker-supplied \"zip\" header at line 452. Decompression occurs before the cek_valid gate at line 189. The authentication at line 240 is genuinely not a barrier: constants.py:70 includes RSA_KW in ALGORITHMS.SUPPORTED, and RSA_KW = {RSA1_5, RSA-OAEP, RSA-OAEP-256} (constants.py:54), so any party holding only the recipient's PUBLIC key can mint a correctly-authenticated JWE carrying a highly-compressed payload and the tag check passes legitimately. Graph confirms exploitability: lines 79/178/187/452/453 are all reachable_from_entry=true with hop_distance=1 (zlib.decompress uid 37f07b7e8019228b7edca4c498c6ff78372e6264), and jose/jwe.py:<module>.decrypt (line 60) is an EntryPoint with exposure='reachable', kind='handler'; jwe.decrypt is the documented public API (docs/jwe/index.rst:70). TWO CORRECTIONS to the analyst: (1) ECDH-ES is NOT in ALGORITHMS.SUPPORTED -- constants.py:70 excludes EC_KW, which appears only in ALL (line 72); the public-key argument survives on RSA_KW alone so the conclusion is unaffected. (2) \"~50KB expands to multiple GB\" is overstated -- zlib's maximum ratio is ~1032:1, so 50KB of ciphertext yields ~50MB; multi-GB requires a multi-MB token, which is still achievable because nothing bounds jwe_str length. Version pinned at 3.3.0 (jose/__init__.py:1) with CHANGELOG.md:3 showing \"3.4.0 -- UNPUBLISHED\" containing only \"Remove support for python 3.6\", matching CVE-2024-33664 (JWT bomb, affects python-jose through 3.3.0, fixed in 3.4.0); the 3.4.0 size-limit fix is demonstrably absent. Caveat: exploit_search returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\", so the CVE mapping rests on my own source reading plus the pinned version, not on an exploit-module citation. Severity MEDIUM: memory-exhaustion DoS only, no auth bypass or code execution, and it requires the consuming application to call jwe.decrypt on untrusted input."
          },
