        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-347",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "JWS/JWT algorithm-confusion via an INCOMPLETE HMAC-secret blocklist, enabling signature forgery. Attacker-controlled `alg` is read from the untrusted token header at jose/jws.py:250 (`alg = header.get(\"alg\")`) and flows unsanitized into key construction: jose/jws.py:257 `_get_keys(key)` -> jose/jws.py:208 `jwk.construct(key, alg)` -> jose/jwk.py:76-79 `key_class = get_key(algorithm); key_class(key_data, algorithm)` -> jose/backends/native.py:23 `HMACKey.__init__(key, algorithm)`. HMACKey guards against using an asymmetric public key as an HMAC secret with a hardcoded blocklist at jose/backends/native.py:39, but that list is only `[b\"-----BEGIN PUBLIC KEY-----\", b\"-----BEGIN RSA PUBLIC KEY-----\", b\"-----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----\", b\"ssh-rsa\"]` - it OMITS the OpenSSH ECDSA prefixes (`ecdsa-sha2-nistp256`/`nistp384`/`nistp521`) and Ed25519 (`ssh-ed25519`), as well as `ssh-dss`. An application that verifies tokens using an OpenSSH-format EC/Ed25519 public key (e.g. an `authorized_keys`-style blob) while HS256 is reachable will construct `HMACKey(b\"ecdsa-sha2-nistp256 AAAA...\", \"HS256\")`; the blocklist check at line 46 (`any(string_value in key for string_value in invalid_strings)`) does not match, so line 52 sets `self.prepared_key = key` and the PUBLIC key becomes the HMAC secret. An attacker who knows that public key can then mint a token with `alg: HS256` and a valid HMAC, forging arbitrary authenticated claims.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Independently re-derived and confirmed. The HMAC-secret blocklist that exists specifically to stop asymmetric public keys being used as HMAC secrets contains only 4 entries, and `ssh-rsa` is the only SSH key type covered; a repo-wide grep for `ecdsa-sha2|ssh-ed25519|ssh-dss|BEGIN OPENSSH` returns zero hits, so the omission is total and unpatched. The attacker fully controls `alg`: jws.py:250 reads it from the header JSON-parsed at jws.py:185 out of the untrusted token before any signature verification, and the only gate (jws.py:254) is opt-in -- it fires solely when `algorithms is not None`, while jose/jwt.py:55 declares `decode(token, key, algorithms=None, ...)`, so `jwt.decode(token, key)` imposes no algorithm restriction whatsoever. The taint reaches HMACKey via jws.py:257 `_get_keys` -> jws.py:208 `jwk.construct(key, alg)` -> jwk.py:76-79 `get_key(algorithm)`/`key_class(key_data, algorithm)`, with jwk.py:34-35 routing any HMAC alg to HMACKey and HS256 in ALGORITHMS.HMAC/SUPPORTED (constants.py:52,70). An app verifying with an OpenSSH-format EC/Ed25519/DSS public key therefore constructs HMACKey(b\"ecdsa-sha2-nistp256 AAAA...\", \"HS256\"), the line-46/562 check misses, prepared_key becomes the PUBLIC key, and anyone who knows that key can mint a valid HS256 token -- full signature forgery. tests/algorithms/test_HMAC.py:15-30 proves the guard is a deliberate control whose gap is simply untested. TWO CORRECTIONS to the analyst: (1) the location was incomplete -- the analyst cited only native.py, the FALLBACK backend, but backends/__init__.py:28 prefers CryptographyHMACKey whenever `cryptography` is installed (the standard install), and that class carries the IDENTICAL incomplete blocklist at cryptography_backend.py:555-568, so the primary path was missed; (2) exploitability is narrower than claimed -- PEM EC public keys ARE blocked by the generic `-----BEGIN PUBLIC KEY-----` entry and CryptographyECKey (cryptography_backend.py:81-96) parses only PEM, so the live gap requires the app to supply OpenSSH-format key bytes, a real but non-default configuration. Version pinned to 3.3.0 (jose/__init__.py:1), inside the CVE-2024-33663 range (\"through 3.3.0\", algorithm confusion with OpenSSH ECDSA keys) with no backported fix in source; exploit_search was unavailable (corpus not indexed), so the version comparison rests on the pinned manifest plus unpatched source. Severity held to MEDIUM rather than HIGH because of the key-format precondition."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "jose/backends/cryptography_backend.py",
                  "uriBaseId": "%SRCROOT%"
                },
