        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-208",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Non-constant-time comparison of the attacker-supplied JWE authentication tag in jose/jwe.py:240 (`if auth_tag != auth_tag_check: raise JWEError(\"Invalid JWE Auth Tag\")`), inside `_decrypt_and_auth`. The `auth_tag` operand is attacker-controlled: it is parsed from the untrusted token at jose/jwe.py:79 (`auth_tag = tmp0[5]` from `_jwe_compact_deserialize(jwe_str)`) and passed into `_decrypt_and_auth` at line 178 (FLOWS_TO arg_index 6), while `auth_tag_check` is the locally computed HMAC from line 232 `_auth_tag(cipher_text, iv, aad, mac_key, key_len)`. Python's `!=` on bytes is a short-circuiting byte-wise comparison, so its execution time is proportional to the length of the matching prefix, leaking the correct tag one byte at a time to an attacker who can time many decrypt attempts - permitting forgery of the AES-CBC-HMAC-SHA2 authentication tag. This is demonstrably an oversight rather than an accepted risk: the same codebase uses the constant-time primitive `hmac.compare_digest(sig, self.sign(msg))` at jose/backends/native.py:69 for JWS signatures. Additionally, line 239 performs `encryption_key.decrypt(cipher_text, iv, aad, auth_tag)` BEFORE the tag comparison at line 240, i.e. decrypt-then-verify ordering on the HMAC_AUTH_TAG path.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Independently re-derived and confirmed against source. jose/jwe.py:240 compares the attacker-supplied JWE authentication tag against the locally computed HMAC using Python's non-constant-time `!=` operator instead of hmac.compare_digest. The tainted operand is genuinely attacker-controlled: _jwe_compact_deserialize base64url-decodes the 5th dot-segment of the untrusted token into auth_tag (jwe.py:323, returned :327), destructured at jwe.py:79 and passed to _decrypt_and_auth at jwe.py:178. The secret operand auth_tag_check is the truncated HMAC from jwe.py:232 -> _auth_tag -> mac_key.sign (jwe.py:571-575). The oversight framing is correct: a repo-wide grep shows jwe.py:240 is the ONLY MAC/tag comparison in the codebase using a bare operator, while jose/backends/native.py:69 correctly uses hmac.compare_digest(sig, self.sign(msg)) for JWS. I found one fact that strengthens the lead beyond the analyst's claim: AESKey.decrypt in cryptography_backend.py:490-515 ignores its `tag` argument entirely on the CBC path (:504-511 only decrypts and PKCS7-unpads; `tag` is consumed only in the GCM branch :495-503), so jwe.py:240 is the SOLE authentication check for all A*CBC-HS* tokens, not a redundant one. The decrypt-then-verify ordering is also real: :239 unpads attacker-chosen ciphertext before :240 authenticates, and the outcomes are caller-distinguishable because cryptography_backend.py:514 wraps the unpad error and jwe.py:181-182 re-raises it as JWEError(e) (\"Invalid padding bytes\") versus jwe.py:241's \"Invalid JWE Auth Tag\" - a CBC padding-oracle surface. Reachability confirmed in both source (public jwe.decrypt -> :178 -> :240) and graph (reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=1). Severity held to MEDIUM rather than HIGH: the timing channel itself is memcmp-scale over a <=32-byte tag, buried under base64/JSON/HMAC-SHA2/AES-CBC work per request, so remote byte-at-a-time tag recovery is impractical in isolation; the MEDIUM rating is driven primarily by the unauthenticated-unpad ordering at the same site. I corrected the location from the analyst's 240-240 to 239-241 to span the decrypt-before-verify. Exploit-reference check could not be performed: the corpus returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\", so no published-module/version-range comparison was made for python-jose 3.3.0 (pinned at jose/__init__.py:1); this was not used as evidence either for or against."
          },
          "locations": [
