        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-863",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "MISSING AUDIENCE ENFORCEMENT when the `aud` claim is absent: `_validate_aud` (jose/jwt.py:316) begins with the test `\"aud\" not in claims` at line 336, and the graph contains NO call nodes at all between line 336 and line 341 -- i.e. that branch performs an unconditional early return with no `raise`, even when the caller explicitly supplied an expected `audience`. The next executed statement is line 341 `audience_claims = claims[\"aud\"]`. Combined with `require_aud` defaulting to False (jose/jwt.py:114), a token that simply omits `aud` is accepted by an application that passes `audience=\"my-api\"`, so a token minted for a different relying party (same issuer/key) is accepted -- cross-audience token replay. The enforcement for the present-`aud` case does exist (line 348 `audience not in audience_claims` -> line 349 `raise JWTClaimsError(\"Invalid audience\")`), which is what makes the absent-`aud` gap an asymmetric hole.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived from source: jose/jwt.py:336-339 `_validate_aud` returns unconditionally when the `aud` claim is absent, with the rejecting `raise` left commented out, so the caller-supplied `audience` argument is silently discarded. Nothing upstream compensates: `_validate_claims` (jwt.py:462-465) only enforces presence for `require_*` options that are truthy, and `require_aud` defaults to False (jwt.py:124), while `verify_aud` defaults True (jwt.py:116) so `_validate_aud` is invoked (jwt.py:481-482) and merely no-ops. The present-`aud` path by contrast does reject (jwt.py:348-349 `raise JWTClaimsError(\"Invalid audience\")`), making the absent-`aud` case an asymmetric authorization gap. The behavior is pinned by the project's own test: tests/test_jwt.py:349-353 `test_aud_empty_claim` decodes a token built from the `{\"a\": \"b\"}` fixture (tests/test_jwt.py:12-14, no `aud`) with `audience=\"audience\"` and asserts success. Reachability is genuine, not an under-linking artifact: jose/jwt.py:336 has reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=1, matching the source path from the public `decode()` entry (jwt.py:156-164). Net effect: a token minted for a different relying party under the same issuer/key but omitting `aud` is accepted by an app that passes `audience=\"my-api\"` \u2014 cross-audience token replay. Severity MEDIUM, not HIGH: RFC 7519 4.1.3 only mandates rejection when `aud` is present, and an opt-in mitigation (`options={\"require_aud\": True}`) exists, so exploitation needs an attacker-obtainable same-key token lacking `aud`. Exploit-reference check could not be performed \u2014 the Metasploit corpus returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\" \u2014 so no patched/unpatched inference is drawn from it; version pinned exactly to python-jose 3.3.0 (jose/__init__.py:1)."
