          "ruleId": "CWE-20",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "NO `crit` HEADER PROCESSING ANYWHERE in the library: RFC 7515 s4.1.11 requires that a recipient REJECT a JWS/JWE whose protected header carries a `crit` member naming extensions the implementation does not understand. A repo-wide search for the string 'crit' across every CpgCall.code in the scan returned zero rows, so neither jose/jws.py's `_verify_signature`/`_load` nor jose/jwe.py's `_jwe_compact_deserialize` inspects it. `_jwe_compact_deserialize` only validates that the header is a JSON Mapping (jose/jwe.py:304-305) and `decrypt` only checks `alg`/`enc` membership (jose/jwe.py:92-98), so a token marking a security-relevant extension as critical is silently accepted and the extension ignored -- defeating any downstream security semantics the sender attached to it.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Independently re-derived and true. A repo-wide case-insensitive grep for 'crit' across the entire tree returns exactly ONE match, and it is a COMMENT (jose/jwe.py:83) -- no code path processes the `crit` header. I reproduced the graph query myself (0 CpgCall rows). Reading jose/jws.py in full: `_load` (172-202) validates only segment splitting, base64 padding and `isinstance(header, Mapping)`; `_verify_signature` (249-264) reads only `alg`. The strongest evidence -- better than the analyst's -- is jose/jwe.py:81-98, where the code transcribes the RFC 7516 s5.2 requirement verbatim as a comment (\"...or by the \\\"crit\\\" Header Parameter value...\") and then implements only `alg`/`enc` membership checks in ALGORITHMS.SUPPORTED. I also ruled out a mitigating strict-header policy: no general unknown-header rejection exists anywhere; only alg/enc/kid/typ are ever read, so `crit` is not mooted. Reachability is NOT a downgrade factor here -- decrypt (L60), _jwe_compact_deserialize (L256), jws.verify (L48), _verify_signature (L249) and jwt.decode (L55) all carry reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=0 as public library API. Three corrections to the analyst: (1) the claimed anchor jwe.py:300-305 is merely where crit ISN'T (just json.loads + Mapping check) -- the real anchor is jwe.py:81-98; (2) CWE-347 is wrong, since signature and AEAD tag verification are fully intact and nothing cryptographic is bypassed -- the correct class is CWE-20; (3) severity is LOW, not as implied: python-jose implements no crit-eligible extensions itself, so an attacker adding `crit` bypasses no in-library check and gains nothing directly. The genuine risk is interop/defense-in-depth -- a producer attaching a security-relevant critical extension has it silently ignored by a python-jose consumer, which RFC 7515 s4.1.11 says MUST be rejected. This fails open on policy metadata, not on crypto. I checked the b64:false (RFC 7797) angle too: it fails closed with JWSError(\"Invalid payload padding\"), not a bypass. Version pinned to python-jose 3.3.0 (jose/__init__.py:1); exploit_search returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\", so I claim neither a weaponized exploit nor patched status from it."
