          "ruleId": "CWE-409",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "PROTECTION APPLIED TOO LATE (ordering): jwe.decrypt computes the `cek_valid` flag early (jose/jwe.py:127/138/144) as the Bleichenbacher/MMA mitigation, but only consults it in the very last statement, line 189 `plain_text if cek_valid else None`. Before that gate is reached, line 187 runs `_decompress(header.get(\"zip\"), plain_text)` on attacker-controlled data with an attacker-controlled `zip` header value. So the full decompression is performed even when the content-encryption key was never validly unwrapped (cek_valid=False, set at line 144), making the zlib decompression reachable on tokens the library has already decided to reject -- unbounded decompression of attacker data (zip-bomb amplification, the python-jose CVE-2024-33664 shape). The guard exists and is simply evaluated after the expensive attacker-driven work rather than before it.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): CONFIRMED as a vulnerability, but the analyst's MECHANISM is WRONG and I am correcting it. (1) REFUTED \u2014 the claimed \"protection applied too late\" ordering is not exploitable: when cek_valid=False is set at jose/jwe.py:144, cek_bytes is replaced by a random CEK (line 152), so _decrypt_and_auth at line 178 necessarily fails \u2014 for GCM, AESGCM.decrypt raises InvalidTag -> JWEError (cryptography_backend.py:502-503, 514-515); for CBC+HMAC, `auth_tag != auth_tag_check` raises JWEError at jwe.py:240-241. Both propagate through the except at jwe.py:181-182, so line 187 `_decompress(...)` is NEVER reached on the cek_valid=False branch. The only route to 187 with cek_valid=False is the `dir` branch (line 127 `cek_valid = encrypted_key == b\"\"`; encrypted_key is not covered by the AAD, line 167 `aad = protected_header`), but reaching 187 there still requires a ciphertext already authenticated under the recipient's real key, so the ordering grants the attacker no capability they lacked. The cek_valid gate is therefore not the defect. (2) CONFIRMED \u2014 the real, independent defect is that `_decompress` performs UNBOUNDED decompression: jose/jwe.py:453 `decompressed = zlib.decompress(compressed)` with no `max_length` and no size cap anywhere in the package (grep for max_length|decompressobj|MAX_|limit across jose/ returns only unrelated RSA constants in rsa_backend.py:52). This is reachable on the NORMAL cek_valid=True path: with an asymmetric alg (e.g. RSA-OAEP) an attacker needs only the recipient's PUBLIC key to mint a fully valid token \u2014 own CEK wrapped to the public key (unwrap_key succeeds, cek_valid=True at line 138), valid auth tag, header `zip: DEF` (ZIPS.SUPPORTED = {DEF, None}, constants.py:92-95) \u2014 whose ~1MB ciphertext expands to gigabytes at line 453, causing memory exhaustion before the line 189 gate is ever relevant. This is CVE-2024-33664 / GHSA-cjwg-qfpm-7377; the tree pins __version__ = \"3.3.0\" (jose/__init__.py:1), inside the affected range (<= 3.3.0, fixed in 3.4.0). The exploit_search corpus returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\", so the version comparison rests on source + advisory range, not on a Metasploit module. The analyst's CWE-409 and sink line 187 are correct; the cek_valid/ordering rationale and the claim that decompression runs on rejected tokens are not."
