          "ruleId": "CWE-347",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Second, independent copy of the same incomplete denylist LITERAL in the cryptography backend: jose/backends/cryptography_backend.py line 555 defines the identical `invalid_strings = [b\"-----BEGIN PUBLIC KEY-----\", b\"-----BEGIN RSA PUBLIC KEY-----\", b\"-----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----\", b\"ssh-rsa\"]` inside CryptographyHMACKey.__init__, enforced at line 562 with `any(string_value in key for string_value in invalid_strings)` and raising JWKError at lines 563-564. Because the list is duplicated rather than shared, fixing only one backend leaves the other exploitable, and this is the backend actually selected when the `cryptography` package is installed (the default install path). Same omissions as the native copy: OpenSSH ECDSA/Ed25519/DSA prefixes and PEM EC/OPENSSH public-key headers all bypass the guard, so an OpenSSH-format EC public key is accepted as an HMAC secret and the key is assigned to self.prepared_key at line 568.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived independently. The exact denylist literal, its enforcement, and the unguarded assignment are present verbatim in CryptographyHMACKey.__init__ at jose/backends/cryptography_backend.py:555-568, and it is a true second copy of the identical 4-entry list in jose/backends/native.py:39-50 (grep shows only these two sites; no shared constant), so patching one backend leaves the other exploitable. This is the backend actually used by default: jose/backends/__init__.py:27-30 binds HMACKey = CryptographyHMACKey and only falls back to native on ImportError. The guard is the SOLE mitigation on a fully attacker-influenced path: jwt.decode defaults algorithms=None (jwt.py:55,141), _verify_signature only enforces the header alg when algorithms is not None (jws.py:249-255), and _sig_matches_keys calls jwk.construct(key, alg) with the attacker-chosen alg (jws.py:208), which jwk.get_key maps HS256->HMACKey (jwk.py:34-35). The list omits the OpenSSH wire-format prefixes ecdsa-sha2-nistp256/384/521, ssh-ed25519 and ssh-dss, so such a public key passes line 562 and is stored as the HMAC secret at line 568 -- the classic asymmetric-to-HMAC algorithm-confusion token-forgery primitive. Version is pinned to python-jose 3.3.0 (jose/__init__.py:1) with CHANGELOG.md:3 showing \"3.4.0 -- UNPUBLISHED\" and no security entry, i.e. pre-fix and squarely inside the CVE-2024-33663 range (\"python-jose through 3.3.0 ... algorithm confusion with OpenSSH ECDSA keys and other key formats\"), whose upstream fix was exactly extending this denylist. exploit_search could not be used as corroboration (corpus returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\"), so the version comparison rests on the in-repo version string alone. Two corrections to the analyst: PEM EC public keys use \"-----BEGIN PUBLIC KEY-----\" which IS blocked and no \"BEGIN OPENSSH PUBLIC KEY\" header exists, so only the OpenSSH wire prefixes are real omissions; and exploitability is narrower than claimed because jose's own EC/RSA loaders are PEM-only (cryptography_backend.py:87,265; ecdsa_backend.py:65), so the attack needs a mixed/multi-key store (jws.py:217-246) plus an unconstrained algorithms argument. Severity MEDIUM rather than HIGH for that reason."
