        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-1395",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Declared dependency `ecdsa` uses the version specifier LITERAL `!= 0.15`, an exclusion with no lower bound, so dependency resolution may install arbitrarily old and known-vulnerable releases (e.g. 0.13.x, affected by CVE-2019-14853 malformed-signature DoS and CVE-2019-14859 signature-malleability). This pin shape is the defect: it blacklists exactly one bad version instead of requiring a minimum safe one. The pure-Python `ecdsa` library is used for real EC signature verification in this repo (jose/backends/ecdsa_backend.py maps NIST256p/NIST384p/NIST521p curves and constructs ecdsa.ellipticcurve.Point / ecdsa.SigningKey / ecdsa.VerifyingKey), and it additionally carries CVE-2024-23342 (Minerva timing side-channel on ECDSA nonce handling) for which upstream ships no fix, so ES256/ES384/ES512 signing here is side-channel exposed regardless of the resolved version.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): The pin-shape defect is present in the source exactly as described, and I verified it independently. setup.cfg:35 declares `ecdsa != 0.15` in install_requires -- an exclusion-only specifier with NO lower bound. This is anomalous within its own block: the two sibling requirements on lines 36-37 both carry floors (`rsa >=4.0, <5.0, !=4.4, !=4.1.1` and `pyasn1 >=0.4.1, <0.5.0`), so the missing floor is not a project-wide style. requirements.txt:3 repeats the same specifier, and the graph confirms :Dependency{name:'ecdsa', version:'!= 0.15'}. I searched the entire tree (pyproject.toml, requirements-dev.txt, requirements-rtd.txt, tox.ini) for any other ecdsa bound and found none; there is no lockfile and no vendored copy, so the floor is genuinely unbounded.\n\nThe dependency is load-bearing and reachable, so this is not a dead pin: jose/jwk.py:40-43 routes every ALGORITHMS.EC algorithm to ECKey; jose/backends/__init__.py:17-20 resolves ECKey to the pure-Python ecdsa backend whenever `cryptography` import fails; and `cryptography` is only an OPTIONAL extra (setup.cfg:43-44), so a default `pip install python-jose` uses this backend for ES256/ES384/ES512. The graph shows ECDSAECKey.__init__, _process_jwk, sign and verify all with reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=0, and ecdsa_backend.py:98-109 performs real signing/verification via the pure-Python library.\n\nTwo corrections to the analyst. (1) The claimed location jose/backends/ecdsa_backend.py:25-33 is the USAGE site, not the defect; a pin-shape defect lives in the manifest, so the location is setup.cfg:35 (secondary: requirements.txt:3). (2) The analyst cited uid b6342736d2e1512e55bf4cce29d760a7312171c7 as line 95 / 'ecdsa.ellipticcurve.Point(...)'; the graph actually places that uid at line 137 ('key_size = self.prepared_key.curve.baselen'). The Point(...) call does exist, but at line 95 under a different node -- substance holds, one citation was mislabelled.\n\nScope limits I will not paper over. exploit_search returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\", so I grounded NO published exploit module. With no lockfile I cannot pin an exact resolved ecdsa version, so per the version-pinning rule exploitability of CVE-2019-14853 / CVE-2019-14859 (both fixed in ecdsa 0.13.3) is UNKNOWN rather than demonstrated: pip's default resolution selects the newest compatible release and lands on an already-patched build. The unbounded floor only materialises under resolver backtracking, external constraint pressure, or a pre-pinned old environment -- a real but conditional risk. CVE-2024-23342 (Minerva timing side-channel, no upstream fix) is version-independent, and I verified its structural precondition myself in non-constant-time pure-Python signing at ecdsa_backend.py:98-101, but exploiting it requires a co-resident attacker with fine-grained timing access to a signing oracle.\n\nI therefore confirm the shape-D pin defect at LOW severity -- a genuine dependency-resolution hygiene / supply-chain hardening issue, not a directly exploitable code flaw."
